##Authority=VCFv4.3.tex:117 (see row 1's fixture) -- the attribute list splits at every raw, unquoted comma with no exception for one inside angle brackets (unlike htslib's own nesting counter, which this library deliberately does not implement -- see AGENTS.md's angle-bracket worked example). The interior comma in this unquoted value ends the Extra attribute early; the second half becomes an unparseable, name-less fragment that is dropped. The surviving "A<1" half also still contains a raw '<', so a second, distinct warning fires for it: InternalUtils.parseOneAttribute's structural-character warning (InternalUtils.java:504-505), already recorded as a parse-time outcome by STRUCTURAL_DELIMITER_HANDLING.md's Row 31 on 2026-08-15 (see also the unescaped-less-than/-greater-than fixtures). Both warnings are pinned below.
##ExpectWarnContains=has no '='; dropping it
##ExpectWarnContains=containing a character bcftools treats as structural; preserving it
##RoundTripDivergenceReason=InternalUtils.parseOneAttribute splits the unquoted Extra value on its interior comma and drops the name-less "2>B" fragment (the ##ExpectWarnContains= warning above); the writer then re-emits only the surviving "A<1" half of Extra, so the round trip is not byte-identical -- verified live during authoring: the written ##INFO line reads Extra=A<1 instead of the original Extra=A<1,2>B.
